course: biology
lesson: 251
title: Plants deeper: leaves and sunlight, how a tree is made of air
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A leaf is a tiny food factory. It takes in carbon dioxide gas from the air through little holes, pulls up water from the roots, and uses the energy of sunlight to join them into sugar. This is called photosynthesis.
explain: The green stuff in leaves, chlorophyll, is what catches the light. The leftover from the factory is oxygen, the gas we breathe.
explain: Here is the surprise: a huge oak does not eat soil to grow. Most of its wood is made from carbon taken out of the air, so in a real way a tree is built from air and water with sunlight as the power.
explain: Put a pondweed leaf in a glass of water on a sunny windowsill and watch: tiny bubbles of oxygen rise from it, the factory working in front of your eyes.
fact: Leaves use sunlight to turn water and carbon dioxide from the air into sugar for food; this is called photosynthesis.
fact: Most of a tree's weight comes from carbon dioxide in the air turned into wood, not from the soil.
fact: In the 1600s Jan van Helmont grew a willow in a pot for five years; the tree gained a lot of weight but the soil lost hardly any.
fact: In autumn many trees stop making green chlorophyll and take it back, so yellow and orange colours, there all along, show through; reds are made fresh, brightest after sunny days and cold nights.
fact: The underside of a leaf has tiny holes called stomata that open to let air in and water vapour out; a big tree can lose hundreds of litres of water on a hot day.
fact: Seaweeds and tiny algae in the sea also photosynthesise, and ocean algae and similar tiny sea life make roughly half of the world's oxygen.
fact: The Amazon giant water lily has leaves up to about three metres across, held up by stiff ribs underneath, strong enough to hold a small child spread on a board.
fact: The white patches of a variegated leaf have no chlorophyll and make no food, so such plants often grow more slowly.
fact: The sensitive plant, mimosa, folds its leaflets within seconds when touched, by moving water out of cells at the base of each leaf.
fact: In one experiment, mimosa plants dropped again and again without harm stopped folding and still «remembered» weeks later; scientists still argue about whether to call this learning.
fact: Young sunflowers turn from east to west with the sun during the day and swing back at night; grown flower heads stay facing east, which warms them in the morning and brings more bees.
fact: Pine needles are thin, tough and waxy, so they lose little water in winter when the ground is frozen; some bristlecone pine needles stay on the tree for over 40 years.
fact: The bubbles from a water plant in sunlight are oxygen; in the 1770s Joseph Priestley found that a plant could «restore» stale air in a closed jar.
exercise: What is photosynthesis? | A leaf using sunlight to make sugar from water and air | A leaf sucking its food up out of the soil through the stalk | A leaf drinking rain to grow bigger | A leaf breathing in oxygen to keep warm
exercise: Where does most of the material in a tree's wood come from? | Carbon dioxide taken from the air | Soil eaten by the roots | Minerals dissolved in rainwater | Dead leaves that fell around it
exercise: What did van Helmont find after growing a willow in a pot for five years? | The tree got much heavier but the soil hardly changed | The soil lost exactly as much weight as the tree had gained | The tree only grew when he added more soil | The tree and the soil lost weight together
exercise: Why do leaves turn yellow and orange in autumn? | The green chlorophyll is taken back and the hidden colours show | The cold paints the leaves yellow overnight | The leaves slowly fill up with orange rust from all the autumn rain | The leaves are burned by the low autumn sun
exercise: What gives the brightest red autumn leaves? | Sunny days followed by cold nights | Long rainy weeks with no sun | Warm, cloudy nights all autumn | Strong winds shaking the branches
exercise: What are stomata? | Tiny holes under a leaf that let air in and water out | Tiny roots that grow out from under the leaf to drink the dew | Small seeds hidden under the leaf | Tiny bugs that live on leaves
exercise: About how much of the world's oxygen comes from tiny algae and other life in the sea? | About half | Almost none | Exactly one tenth | All of it
exercise: How can a giant water lily leaf hold up a small child on a board? | Stiff ribs under the leaf spread the weight | The water underneath it is frozen solid all year | The leaf is made of thin wood | The leaf is full of glue
exercise: Why do variegated plants with white patches often grow more slowly? | The white parts have no chlorophyll and make no food | The white parts suck food from the green parts all day long | The white parts let in too much light | The white parts are where the plant sleeps
exercise: How does a mimosa fold its leaves when touched? | It moves water out of cells at the base of each leaflet | It has tiny muscles inside each leaf, like the muscles in an arm | It shuts because it feels afraid | It closes because the touch makes it cold
exercise: What happened in the experiment where mimosas were dropped again and again without harm? | They stopped folding and still did so weeks later | They folded faster and faster every single time they were dropped | They all died after a few drops | They grew thorns to protect themselves
exercise: What do young sunflowers do during a day? | Turn from east to west with the sun, then back at night | Stay facing south all day and all night | Spin round and round all night long, like little windmills | Face away from the sun to keep cool
exercise: Why can a pine keep its needles through winter? | The thin, waxy needles lose little water when the ground is frozen | The needles are full of antifreeze, like the water in a car engine | The pine grows new needles every single night | Snow sticks to the needles and keeps them warm
exercise: What are the tiny bubbles rising from a water plant in the sun? | Oxygen made by photosynthesis | Carbon dioxide the plant does not want | Air that was trapped in the glass | Water boiling in the heat of the sun
exercise: What did Joseph Priestley find about a plant in a closed jar? | It could restore stale air so it was fit to breathe | It quickly made the air poisonous for the mouse | It died at once without any soil | It made the air in the jar get hotter and hotter until it boiled
test: «my mate says trees grow by eating the dirt, that's why they need soil innit» What is right? | No, most of a tree is made from carbon dioxide from the air | Yes, a tree is mostly soil packed tightly into wood by the roots | Yes, roots chew the dirt like teeth | No, a tree is made mostly of rainwater
test: What would happen if a leaf had all its stomata blocked with grease? | It could not take in carbon dioxide, so it could not make food | It would make food faster than before | It would turn bright red at once | Nothing at all, because leaves do not need any air to make their food
test: You put one pondweed in the sun and one in a dark cupboard, both in water. Which makes more bubbles? | The one in the sun | The one in the cupboard | Both exactly the same | Neither, pondweed never makes bubbles
test: Count them: 3 sunflowers each have 1 flower head and 12 leaves. How many leaves and heads altogether? | 36 leaves and 3 heads | 12 leaves and 3 heads | 36 leaves and 36 heads | 15 leaves and 1 head
test: «autumn's here so the tree paints its leaves yellow, cool» What is a better way to say it? | The yellow was there all summer, hidden under the green | The tree makes yellow paint out of the soil and sends it up | The frost colours the leaves overnight | The yellow comes from the sun burning the leaves
test: In a cartoon, Jerry hides in a pot plant and the plant gives him a sandwich it made from soil. What is true of real plants? | They make sugar for themselves from air, water and light, not sandwiches from soil | They really cook food from soil for the animals that live in the forest | They make food only at night in the dark | They do not need any food at all
test: Which is true about the oxygen you breathe? | It was given off by plants and algae as a leftover of making food | It comes out of the ground from volcanoes | It is made by clouds when it rains | It was always in the air since the Earth began and is never made by anything
test: A gardener keeps a plant with white and green leaves next to an all-green one in the same pot size and light. What will most likely happen? | The all-green one grows faster | The white and green one grows faster | They grow at exactly the same speed | The white parts turn green by next week
test: «poked the mimosa like 50 times and it got lazy and stopped closing, broken?» What is the likely answer? | Not broken: mimosas touched again and again without harm can stop folding | Yes, it is dying and must be thrown away | Yes, the leaves have run out of water forever and will never move again | No, it is just pretending to be asleep
test: Why would a broad oak leaf be a problem in a frozen winter? | It would lose water the roots cannot replace from frozen ground | It would freeze into a solid block of ice and explode | It would make too much food in the snow | It would catch too much sunlight in winter
test: In a game, a plant character drinks sunlight like juice from a glass. What is closer to the truth? | Sunlight is the energy that powers a leaf's food making, not something it drinks | Leaves really store the sunlight like juice inside them, to drink later | Plants do not use sunlight at all | Only flowers use light, never leaves
test: «so if half our oxygen is from the sea, we breathe thanks to seaweed and tiny algae?» What is the answer? | Yes, a large part comes from tiny algae and other sea life | No, all oxygen comes from rainforests | No, the sea takes oxygen away and never makes it | Only from fish breathing out bubbles
exam: Identify the principal source of the carbon which constitutes the greater part of the dry mass of a tree. | Carbon dioxide absorbed from the atmosphere | Mineral matter extracted from the soil by the roots | Organic matter derived from fallen leaves | Water drawn up through the roots
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the colouration of deciduous leaves in autumn. | Yellow and orange pigments, present throughout, are revealed when chlorophyll is withdrawn | Yellow pigment is manufactured afresh in response to frost | The colouration results from rust deposited by rainfall | The colouration is produced by solar scorching of the leaf during the shortening days of autumn
exam: State the function of the minute apertures, hereinafter referred to as stomata, situated upon the underside of a leaf. | The admission of air and the release of water vapour | The absorption of mineral salts from rainwater | The secretion of nectar for pollinating insects | The anchorage of the leaf to the stem
exam: It is given that a submerged aquatic plant, exposed to sunlight, emits small bubbles. Ascertain the identity of the gas so emitted. | Oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Nitrogen | Water vapour
